Let's produce.</think>The Fourier transform \(X(j\omega)\) of  
\[
x(t)=\left(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t}\right)^2
\]
has support up to
\[
\omega_{\max}=100\pi.
\]
This is because \(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t}\) has bandwidth \(50\pi\), and squaring in time doubles the bandwidth.

The sampling frequency is
\[
\omega_s=150\pi.
\]
For the sampled spectrum,
\[
G(j\omega)=\frac{1}{T}\sum_k X(j(\omega-k\omega_s)),
\]
and \(\frac{1}{T}=75\). Thus
\[
G(j\omega)=75\sum_k X(j(\omega-k\omega_s)).
\]

To have \(G(j\omega)=75X(j\omega)\) for \(|\omega|\le \omega_0\), no shifted copies may overlap the baseband. The first shifted copy begins at
\[
\omega_s-\omega_{\max}=150\pi-100\pi=50\pi.
\]
Therefore the maximum allowable \(\omega_0\) is \(50\pi\).

