\[
AB=x=28\cos\alpha.
\]
Similarly, since \(AL=CL=14\),
\[
AC=y=28\cos\beta.
\]
Also \(AK=AL=KL=14\), so \(\triangle AKL\) is equilateral and
\[
\angle KAL=60^\circ.
\]
Because \(\angle BAC=90^\circ\), we get
\[
\alpha+\beta=30^\circ.
\]

Using \(BC=38\),
\[
(28\cos\alpha)^2+(28\cos\beta)^2=38^2,
\]
so
\[
\cos^2\alpha+\cos^2\beta=\frac{361}{196}.
\]
But
\[
\cos^2\alpha+\cos^2\beta
=1+\cos(\alpha+\beta)\cos(\alpha-\beta)
=1+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\cos(\alpha-\beta).
\]
Thus
\[
\cos(\alpha-\beta)=\frac{165}{98\sqrt3}.
\]

Now the area of \(BKLC\) is
\[
[ABC]-[ABK]-[ACL]-[AKL].
\]
Compute:
\[
[ABC]=392\cos\alpha\cos\beta,
\]
\[
[ABK]=196\sin\alpha\cos\alpha,\quad [ACL]=196\sin\beta\cos\beta,
\]
\[
[AKL]=49\sqrt3.
\]
Therefore
\[
[BKLC]
=392\cos\alpha\cos\beta-196(\sin\alpha\cos\alpha+\sin\beta\cos\beta)-49\sqrt3.
\]
Using \(\alpha+\beta=30^\circ\),
\[
[BKLC]=49\sqrt3+98\cos(\alpha-\beta).
\]
Substitute \(\cos(\alpha-\beta)=\frac{165}{98\sqrt3}\):
\[
[BKLC]=49\sqrt3+\frac{165}{\sqrt3}
=49\sqrt3+55\sqrt3
=104\sqrt3.
\]
Thus \(n=104\).

